# examples/regressions/native_union_known_stop — THE NATIVE BACKEND'S NULL-UNION STOP, PINNED.
#
# Owner ruling 2026-07-28: *"é trocar a asserção para esperar por falha honesta e não skippar ou
# comentar, perfeito. Pode providenciar, atacamos na .32."*
#
# WHY A WHOLE PROJECT FOR ONE SCENARIO, and it is not tidiness — the two existing homes both
# structurally refuse it:
#
#   `bulk.tkr`         every scenario runs off ONE SUCCESSFUL build. A shape that cannot be lowered
#                      does not merely fail its own row, it takes the other 196 with it — which is
#                      exactly what it did, hiding every one of them behind a single line.
#   `diagnostics.tkr`  every scenario runs off ONE FAILING build, but those ~70 cases fail in the
#                      CHECKER. The build dies there and never reaches the backend, so a stop pinned
#                      here would never fire: a test that cannot fail is not a test.
#
# So it gets its own project, whose single build fails in the BACKEND with nothing else competing to
# fail first.
#
# NOT COMMENTED, NOT SKIPPED, and that distinction is the whole point. A commented fixture is a
# silent skip wearing a different hat — it stops asking the question, and nothing ever tells you the
# answer changed. This scenario keeps ASSERTING, on every run: it demands the exact stop the backend
# produces today.
#
# THE DAY THE .32 CLOSES IT, THIS GOES RED. The build will succeed, `When compilation fails` will not
# be satisfied, and the suite will say so loudly. That failure is the SIGNAL to promote this back to
# what it was — `When built and run` / `Then exit = 42` — and move the source back into `bulk`. The
# same shape `diagnostics.tkr` already uses for its own known-stop: *"promote to a positive scenario
# the day it closes"*.
#
# WHAT IS ACTUALLY BROKEN, measured (`examples/probes/match_shapes`, 29 shapes x 3 compilers):
#
#   - the stop is the CAST TARGET asking `prim_kind_of` for `u64 | null`, not the arithmetic — the
#     probe's `m03` has no arithmetic at all and stops identically;
#   - annotating the binding changes NOTHING (the `tNN` control group is identical to `mNN`), so the
#     checker's inference is not involved: the match's type really is `u64`;
#   - `bool | null` and `Box | null` LOWER today, so it is specific to a numeric member;
#   - the published 0.3.0.30 stops on this too, and on six shapes this train has since FIXED. The
#     gap has an origin, not an age — there is no regression to bisect.
#
# The root cause is named in `docs/design/null-as-union-type.md` terms: the C emitter picks a
# representation per declared type (`UnionRepr` = Niche | InlineTag | BoxInArena, keyed off
# `cg_type_is_niche_able` + `cg_type_min_bytes`), while the native backend has exactly one shape —
# `tag@0 + payload@8`, held by address. Porting that choice is the `.32` work.
Feature: native backend — a numeric null-union has no single PrimKind (KNOWN-STOP, .32)

  # src/probe.tks — the body authored 2026-07-24 (`1201088`), unchanged since, and green through
  # the C route the whole time. Only the native backend refuses it.
  Scenario: a `to` cast whose match subject is a numeric null-union stops honestly
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "has no single PrimKind, asked by the cast target"
